digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56853-3
  rankdir="TB";
  "Imp1" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: AD is a major pathological burden with defined hallmarks."];
  "Imp10" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: Proteomics helps + Proteomics limited = Need for deeper proteomics."];
  "Imp2" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: AD has complex molecular drivers beyond Aβ and tau."];
  "Imp3" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: AD is a complex disease requiring resolution of multifactorial mechanisms."];
  "Imp4" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: Models are intended to clarify mechanisms but understanding remains incomplete."];
  "Imp5" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: Current model landscape is poorly characterized and insufficient."];
  "Imp6" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Complex disease + Incomplete understanding = Critical need for better model insight."];
  "Imp8" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Omics technologies include transcriptomics which shows gene changes."];
  "Imp9" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: RNA!=Protein + Inconsistency observed = Transcriptomics is insufficient alone."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper aims to develop and evaluate a deep, age-resolved proteomic, phosphoproteomic, and protein-turnover profiling resource across Alzheimer’s disease mouse models, compare it with human AD datasets, identify shared molecular pathways, examine transcript-protein inconsistencies, and test how amyloid formation alters protein degradation to guide evaluation and selection of AD models.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When AD models are incompletely characterized and transcriptomic measurements can diverge from protein-level biology, integrated protein-focused profiling and human comparison can support model evaluation."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Because transcriptomics alone can miss protein-level changes and earlier proteomics was useful but limited, deeper proteomic profiling is warranted rather than proteomics being a sole solution."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) The need for improved AD model evaluation and the warrant for deeper proteomic profiling together motivate deep proteomic evaluation of AD mouse models."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) The study design includes deep age-dependent protein and phosphopeptide analysis across AD models, additional model profiling, human-mouse comparisons, and analysis of transcript-protein inconsistency."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) A study design that combines deep proteomic and phosphoproteomic analyses with model profiling, human-mouse comparisons, and inconsistency analysis is positioned to address the AD model-evaluation gap."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Protein-turnover profiling in the study assesses how amyloid formation is associated with delayed degradation of amyloidome components."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) The study reports shared AD pathways and altered protein turnover from comprehensive proteomic analysis, and makes the resulting data freely available through a searchable website."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) The study's outputs combine pathway identification, protein-turnover findings linked to amyloid-associated degradation changes, and an accessible data resource for AD model research."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) The poorly characterized AD mouse-model landscape, together with the critical need for better model insight, motivates improved evaluation of AD models."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder affecting >6M Americans."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Transcriptomic analyses revealed changes in immune, synaptic, and neuronal signaling genes."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) RNA levels do not always align with protein levels due to posttranscriptional processes."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) Notable inconsistencies between transcript and protein levels in AD and 5xFAD mice were observed."];
  "S13" [label="(13,0,0) Proteomic studies corroborated transcriptomic findings and identified RNA-independent alterations."];
  "S14" [label="(14,0,0) Early proteomic studies were limited by inadequate depth and insufficient human comparison."];
  "S15" [label="(15,0,0) We present a deep, age-dependent analysis of proteins and phosphopeptides across AD models."];
  "S16" [label="(16,0,0) We profiled additional models, performed human-mouse comparisons, and analyzed inconsistency."];
  "S17" [label="(17,0,0) We measured turnover rates and found amyloid formation delays degradation of amyloidome components."];
  "S18" [label="(18,0,0) Comprehensive proteomic analysis identifies shared AD pathways and altered protein turnover."];
  "S19" [label="(19,0,0) All data are freely available and searchable through an interactive website."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) AD pathology initiates decades before symptoms, defined by Aβ plaques and tau tangles."];
  "S3" [label="(3,0,0) Other molecular changes (α-synuclein, TDP-43, U1 snRNP) may play important roles."];
  "S4" [label="(4,0,0) Genetic analyses elucidated causative, high-risk, and low-risk genes/loci."];
  "S5" [label="(5,0,0) Molecular mechanisms are not fully understood, often due to lack of suitable models."];
  "S6" [label="(6,0,0) More than 100 genetic AD mouse models have been developed."];
  "S7" [label="(7,0,0) None of these models capture the full spectrum of AD molecular events and pathologies."];
  "S8" [label="(8,0,0) No comprehensive resources are available to understand model advantages and limitations."];
  "S9" [label="(9,0,0) Omics technologies provide an opportunity to evaluate disease models on a global scale."];
